b":516.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":190.66666666666666,"r":202.0,"t":525.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/31","text":"16"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Eb/No[dB]","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":499.6666666666667,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":123.66666666666667,"r":167.0,"t":511.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,9],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/32","text":"Eb/No[dB]"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Mbps","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":692.3915914217384,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":186.9969969363352,"r":206.0030030636648,"t":703.6084085782616},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/33","text":"Mbps"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"requires Eb/No of about 14dB for the conventional cor relation receiver; and Eb/No of about 8dB for the four finger RAKE receiver: In addition; a further simulation was done by combining the cluster 1 of the on-body channel and the IEEE8O2.15.3a CMI channel [9] for simulating an additional indoor propagation characteristic. The BER degradation compared with the influence from the metal floor is only IdB at BER 0.01 . These results suggest that the high-speed transmission of 50 Mbps is possible in the onbody communication situation by the use of the RAKE receiver. Since the transmission of 10Mbps may be enough when limiting it to the application of the healthcare and medical treatment, the influence of the multipath will be weakened and a further improvement of the BER perfor mance can be expected. speed","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":288.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":46.333333333333336,"r":289.3333333333333,"t":466.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,814],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/34","text":"requires Eb/No of about 14dB for the conventional cor relation receiver; and Eb/No of about 8dB for the four finger RAKE receiver: In addition; a further simulation was done by combining the cluster 1 of the on-body channel and the IEEE8O2.15.3a CMI channel [9] for simulating an additional indoor propagation characteristic. The BER degradation compared with the influence from the metal floor is only IdB at BER 0.01 . These results suggest that the high-speed transmission of 50 Mbps is possible in the onbody communication situation by the use of the RAKE receiver. Since the transmission of 10Mbps may be enough when limiting it to the application of the healthcare and medical treatment, the influence of the multipath will be weakened and a further improvement of the BER perfor mance can be expected. speed"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"6. Conclusions","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":267.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":48.666666666666664,"r":120.0,"t":278.0},"charspan":[0,14],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/35","text":"6. Conclusions"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"This study aimed to contain the environment reflection in a dynamic on-body UWB channel model.  In addition to various human body postures, the reflection from the environment such as the floor was included. It has been shown how that the environment reflection does not basically affect the multipath components in the first cluster that are generated by the diffraction and reflection of the human body. It is available to establish a body area channel model by a combination of the on-body model and the additional components from the environment. Using the impulse response expression of the channel model generated in such way, the average BER performance of a typical IR-UWB system has been clarified. It has been found that the degradation at BER 0.01 is 2-3dB due to the influence of the reflected waves from the environment. high-speed transmission of 50 Mbps on the body surface is possible by the use of the RAKE receiver structure .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":209.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":47.333333333333336,"r":287.6666666666667,"t":255.6183752844272},"charspan":[0,229],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":562.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":307.3333333333333,"r":549.6666666666666,"t":712.3333333333334},"charspan":[230,944],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/36","text":"This study aimed to contain the environment reflection in a dynamic on-body UWB channel model.  In addition to various human body postures, the reflection from the environment such as the floor was included. It has been shown how that the environment reflection does not basically affect the multipath components in the first cluster that are generated by the diffraction and reflection of the human body. It is available to establish a body area channel model by a combination of the on-body model and the additional components from the environment. Using the impulse response expression of the channel model generated in such way, the average BER performance of a typical IR-UWB system has been clarified. It has been found that the degradation at BER 0.01 is 2-3dB due to the influence of the reflected waves from the environment. high-speed transmission of 50 Mbps on the body surface is possible by the use of the RAKE receiver structure ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"References","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":541.6666666666666,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":308.6666666666667,"r":348.0,"t":549.6666666666666},"charspan":[0,10],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/37","text":"References"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","enumerated":true,"label":"list_item","marker":"[1]","orig":"[1] networks; IEICE Trans. Commun , Feb. 2009.","parent":{"$ref":"#/groups/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":499.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":310.6666666666667,"r":446.0,"t":527.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,46],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#